<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Harz Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Harz is the highest mountain range of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brocken">Brocken</a> (1,141 metres) as its highest peak. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and lead mines, worked from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, enriched towns such as Goslar and the surrounding princely houses. Its forests and fringes were divided among many small territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, including Stolberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wernigerode">Wernigerode</a>, Blankenburg and Anhalt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harz.jpg" alt="Harz" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TerraX_Bln · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ausblick_vom_Grossen_Knollen_(Turm)_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
